IMPORT prelude

-- Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998 (Western Australia) -- registering a child's birth, a marriage and a death,
-- asked by the people the Act is applied to; with the fees set by the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Regulations 1999.
--
-- SEAT: the parent registering a child's birth; the family of a person who has died (and the executor who needs the certificate);
-- and, for Part 6, the couple marrying. Every decision is phrased as one of them would ask it. Where a provision addresses only an
-- official (the hospital, the doctor, the funeral director, the Registrar), it is encoded as what that means for them: who must act,
-- by when, and what they will be given.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 2 with the compilation table's commencement date; s 4 (the definitions used: neonatal death, disposal,
-- prohibited name as the Registrar's opinion, stillborn child as a fact); Part 3: ss 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 19(3)-(4), 22 and 23;
-- Part 6: ss 37-39; Part 7: ss 40, 42, 43, 44, 45, 46, 47 and 48; Part 8: ss 55 and 57 (getting a certificate); s 70 (remission of
-- fees); and the penalties in ss 12, 16, 42, 44, 45, 46 and 47.
--
-- Edition: compilation including the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Amendment (Sex or Gender Changes) Act 2024 Pt 2 (in
-- force 30 May 2025), the last commenced amendment in its compilation table; "(c) State of Western Australia 2025"; as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49099.txt (retrieved 2026-09-07; no compilation number stated). Fees: Births, Deaths and
-- Marriages Registration Regulations 1999, version 01-q0-01, currency from 1 Jul 2026 (last amendment SL 2026/114, in force 1 Jul
-- 2026), as deposited at ../../../births-deaths-and-marriages-registration-regulations-1999/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49611.txt
-- (retrieved 2026-10-19).
--
-- NOT ENCODED: administration (Part 2); court orders beyond their effect as an input (ss 20, 21, 41); parentage changes, adoption
-- and surrogacy (Part 4); change of name (Part 5, except its fee); change of registration of sex or gender and acknowledgment
-- documents (Part 5A); keeping, correcting and amending the Register (ss 49-53); access policies (ss 54, 56, 58); offences and
-- miscellany (Part 9, except s 70); regulations 5-8. The Registrar's approved forms are not deposited. The law before 14 April 1999
-- (Daniel's 1996 registration) is not deposited.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults: the Act with
-- the regulations that set its fees; the actors the text names; a judgment the law hands to the Registrar, a court or a coroner is
-- asked as an input, never guessed; the Act's own definitions worked out from facts. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent
-- test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Dates are written YYYYMMDD as numbers.

§ `Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1998`

§§ `2 -- does this Act govern the event`

-- s 2: "This Act comes into operation on such day as is fixed by proclamation." Compilation table: "Act other than s. 1 and 2: 14 Apr
-- 1999 (see s. 2 and Gazette 9 Apr 1999 p. 1433)". The regulations came into operation the same day (r 2).
@export Does this Act govern the registration of an event on this date?
GIVEN `the date of the event` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the birth, marriage or death, written YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this Act govern the event` IF
    `the date of the event` >= 19990414

§ `Part 3 -- registration of births`

§§ `12 -- notification of a birth by the hospital or attendant`

-- s 12(1) "responsible person": (a) if the child "was born in a hospital or brought to a hospital within 24 hours after the child's
-- birth" -- (i) a public hospital: "the chief executive of the health service provider for that hospital"; (ii) a private hospital:
-- "the chief executive officer or general manager of the hospital"; (b) in any other case -- (i) "the doctor or midwife responsible
-- for the professional care of the child's mother at the birth"; (ii) if stillborn, "the doctor who examined the child's body after
-- the birth"; (iii) if no doctor or midwife attended, "any person in attendance at the birth".
-- Whether a hospital is public (Health Services Act 2016 s 6) or private (Private Hospitals and Health Services Act 1927 s 2(1)) is a
-- borrowed term, asked as a fact.
DECLARE Hospital IS ONE OF
    `a public hospital`
    `a private hospital`
    `no hospital`

DECLARE `Birth notifier` IS ONE OF
    `the chief executive of the health service provider`
    `the hospital's chief executive officer or general manager`
    `the doctor or midwife caring for the mother`
    `the doctor who examined the stillborn child`
    `any person in attendance at the birth`

@export Who must notify the Registrar of my child's birth (s 12)?
GIVEN `the hospital involved` IS A Hospital @desc The hospital where the child was born, or to which the child was brought within 24 hours of birth; no hospital otherwise
      `the child was stillborn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was stillborn (at least 20 weeks or 400 grams, with no sign of life)
      `a doctor or midwife attended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a doctor or midwife attended the birth
GIVETH A `Birth notifier`
DECIDE `Who must notify my child's birth` IS
    CONSIDER `the hospital involved`
    WHEN `a public hospital` THEN `the chief executive of the health service provider`
    WHEN `a private hospital` THEN `the hospital's chief executive officer or general manager`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the child was stillborn`
        THEN `the doctor who examined the stillborn child`
        ELSE IF `a doctor or midwife attended`
             THEN `the doctor or midwife caring for the mother`
             ELSE `any person in attendance at the birth`

-- s 12(2): notice "within one month after the birth". Read as a calendar month, to the same day of the next month (an inference; the
-- Interpretation Act 1984 is not deposited). A day past the end of a shorter month (20300131 + 1 month = 20300231) is not a real date
-- but compares correctly as a number, because no real date lies between it and the first day of the month after.
@export By what date must my child's birth be notified to the Registrar (s 12(2))?
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A NUMBER @desc The child's date of birth, YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `By what date must the birth be notified` IS
    IF `the date of birth` MODULO 10000 >= 1201
    THEN `the date of birth` + 8900
    ELSE `the date of birth` + 100

-- s 12(2) Penalty: $1 000; s 12(3): "A person does not commit an offence under subsection (2) if notice of the birth is given by
-- another person."
@export Was my child's birth notified in time (s 12)?
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A NUMBER @desc The child's date of birth, YYYYMMDD
      `the date of the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the hospital, doctor, midwife or attendant gave the Registrar notice, YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was the birth notified in time` IF
    `the date of the notice` <= `By what date must the birth be notified` `the date of birth`

§§ `13 -- must my child's birth be registered here`

-- s 13(1): "If a child is born in the State, the birth must be registered under this Act." (2): also if a State court orders it.
-- s 4: "State includes a Territory" -- but s 13(1) is read as Western Australia, the State whose Act it is (an inference).
@export Must my child's birth be registered under this Act (s 13(1), (2))?
GIVEN `born in Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was born in Western Australia
      `a State court ordered registration here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a State court has ordered the birth registered in Western Australia
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my child's birth be registered` IF
    `born in Western Australia` OR `a State court ordered registration here`

-- s 13(3): another State's or the Commonwealth's court determination -- MAY "if the Registrar considers that it is appropriate";
-- (4) born in an aircraft or vessel on the way to the State, (5) born outside the Commonwealth to become a resident of the State: MAY;
-- (6) "The Registrar must not register a birth under subsection (4) or (5) if the birth is registered under a corresponding law."
@export May my child's birth be registered here although it need not be (s 13(3)-(6))?
GIVEN `another court determined it should be registered here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court of another State or of the Commonwealth determined that the birth should be registered in Western Australia
      `the Registrar considers it appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar considers it appropriate to register it on that determination
      `born on a flight or voyage to Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was born in an aircraft or vessel travelling to an airport or port in the State
      `born abroad and to become a resident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was born outside Australia and is to become a resident of the State
      `already registered in another State` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the birth is already registered under another State's law
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my child's birth be registered here` IF
        (`another court determined it should be registered here` AND `the Registrar considers it appropriate`)
    OR  (    (`born on a flight or voyage to Western Australia` OR `born abroad and to become a resident`)
         AND (NOT `already registered in another State`))

§§ `15 -- who must have the birth registered`

-- s 15(1): "The parents of a child are jointly responsible for having the child's birth registered under this Act (and must both sign
-- the birth registration statement)". (2): "If a child is a foundling, the person with responsibility for the longterm care, welfare
-- and development of the child is responsible". Nothing in s 15 turns on whether the parents are married.
DECLARE `Birth registrant` IS ONE OF
    `both parents jointly`
    `the foundling's long-term carer`

@export Who is responsible for having my child's birth registered (s 15)?
GIVEN `the child is a foundling` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child is a foundling (found, parents unknown)
GIVETH A `Birth registrant`
DECIDE `Who must have the birth registered` IS
    IF `the child is a foundling` THEN `the foundling's long-term carer` ELSE `both parents jointly`

-- s 15(1) continues: "but the Registrar may accept a birth registration statement from one of the parents if satisfied that it is
-- impracticable for the other parent to join ... because of his or her death, disappearance, ill health or unavailability or the need
-- to avoid unwarranted distress to obtain the signatures of both parents". The Registrar's satisfaction is asked as an input.
DECLARE `Reason the other parent does not sign` IS ONE OF
    `the other parent has died`
    `the other parent has disappeared`
    `the other parent is ill`
    `the other parent is unavailable`
    `asking for both signatures would cause unwarranted distress`
    `no such reason`

@export May I sign the birth registration statement alone (s 15(1))?
GIVEN `the reason` IS A `Reason the other parent does not sign` @desc Why the other parent is not signing
      `the Registrar is satisfied it is impracticable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied that it is impracticable for the other parent to join, for that reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I sign the statement alone` IF
        `the Registrar is satisfied it is impracticable`
    AND CONSIDER `the reason`
        WHEN `no such reason` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `16 -- by when`

-- s 16(1): the person responsible "must ensure that a birth registration statement is lodged with the Registrar within 60 days after
-- the date of the birth." Penalty: $1 000. A statement lodged on day 60 is in time.
DECLARE `Birth registration act` IS ONE OF
    `lodge the birth registration statement`

@export The parents' duty to lodge the birth registration statement within 60 days of the birth (s 16(1))
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Birth registrant` `Birth registration act`
DECIDE `our duty to register the birth` IS
    PARTY `both parents jointly`
    MUST  `lodge the birth registration statement`
    WITHIN 60
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `both parents jointly`

@export How many days after the birth do we have to lodge the birth registration statement (s 16(1))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days do we have to register the birth` IS 60

@export Is our birth registration statement in time (s 16(1))?
GIVEN `days after the birth` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the birth the statement was lodged
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is our statement in time` IF
    `days after the birth` <= `How many days do we have to register the birth`

-- s 16(2): "The Registrar must accept a birth registration statement even though it is lodged after the end of the 60 day period."
@export Must the Registrar still accept our statement if it is late (s 16(2))?
GIVEN `days after the birth` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the birth the statement is lodged
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Registrar accept our statement` IF
    `days after the birth` >= 0

@export What is the most we can be fined for not lodging within 60 days (s 16(1))?
GIVEN `days after the birth` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the birth the statement was (or will be) lodged
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most we can be fined for registering late` IS
    IF `Is our statement in time` `days after the birth` THEN 0 ELSE 1000

§§ `18 -- will both parents be named`

-- s 18(1): the Registrar "is not to include information about the identity of a child's parents in the Register unless" (a) "both
-- parents of the child make a joint application"; (b) one parent applies and the other "cannot join" -- dead, cannot be found, or some
-- other reason; (c) one parent applies and the Registrar "is satisfied that the other parent does not dispute the correctness"; (d) a
-- State court orders it or finds parentage; (e) another court so determines "and the Registrar considers that it is appropriate"; (f)
-- the Registrar may presume parentage under any law. Marriage is not a condition anywhere in s 18.
DECLARE `How the parents apply` IS ONE OF
    `both parents jointly apply`
    `one parent applies and the other cannot join`
    `one parent applies and the other may dispute it`

@export Will both parents be named in the Register (s 18(1))?
GIVEN `the application` IS A `How the parents apply` @desc Whether both parents apply, or one applies alone
      `the Registrar is satisfied the other does not dispute it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied the other parent does not dispute the details
      `a State court ordered it or found the parentage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a State court ordered the details included or found who the parent is
      `another court so determined and the Registrar thinks it appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another State's or the Commonwealth's court so determined and the Registrar considers it appropriate
      `a law lets the Registrar presume the parent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any law entitles the Registrar to presume who the parent is
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will both parents be named` IF
        (CONSIDER `the application`
         WHEN `both parents jointly apply` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `one parent applies and the other cannot join` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE `the Registrar is satisfied the other does not dispute it`)
    OR  `a State court ordered it or found the parentage`
    OR  `another court so determined and the Registrar thinks it appropriate`
    OR  `a law lets the Registrar presume the parent`

§§ `19(3), (4) -- a different surname after registration`

-- s 19(3): the Registrar may enter a proposed surname (a) on a joint application by both parents, or (b) otherwise if "the child's
-- other parent has died" or "cannot be found but other children of the same parents are registered in the proposed surname". (4): only
-- "if the proposed surname is not a prohibited name" and, if the child is 12 or more, the child consents or "is unable to understand
-- the meaning and implications of the change of surname". s 4: a prohibited name is one that "in the opinion of the Registrar" is
-- obscene or offensive, impracticable, or contrary to the public interest -- asked as the Registrar's opinion.
@export May the Registrar change my child's surname on my application (s 19(3), (4))?
GIVEN `both parents apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether both parents make the application together
      `the other parent has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child's other parent has died
      `the other parent is missing and siblings bear the surname` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other parent cannot be found and other children of the same parents are registered in the proposed surname
      `the Registrar thinks the surname prohibited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is of the opinion that the surname is a prohibited name
      `the child's age` IS A NUMBER @desc The child's age in years
      `the child consents or cannot understand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a child of 12 or more: whether the child consents, or cannot understand the change
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my child's surname be changed` IF
        (`both parents apply` OR `the other parent has died` OR `the other parent is missing and siblings bear the surname`)
    AND (NOT `the Registrar thinks the surname prohibited`)
    AND (`the child's age` < 12 OR `the child consents or cannot understand`)

§§ `22 -- the child's name`

-- s 22(1): the statement "must state the name of the child"; (2) not required for "a stillborn child or a child who has a neonatal
-- death" (s 4: "the death of a liveborn child within 28 days after the birth").
@export Must we give our child a name in the birth registration statement (s 22(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the child was stillborn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child was stillborn
      `the child died within 28 days of birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the child, born alive, died within 28 days
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must we name our child` IF
    (NOT `the child was stillborn`) AND (NOT `the child died within 28 days of birth`)

-- s 22(3): "The name is a matter of choice for the person or persons lodging the birth registration statement and nothing in this Act
-- requires that -- (a) the name be made up of both a surname and a given name or given names; or (b) the surname be the same as that of
-- a parent of the child."
@export Must our child's surname be the same as a parent's (s 22(3)(b))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the surname be a parent's` IS FALSE

@export Must our child's name have both a surname and a given name (s 22(3)(a))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the name have a surname and a given name` IS FALSE

-- s 22(4): the Registrar may assign a name if (a) "the name stated ... is a prohibited name", or (b) both parents lodged and "satisfy the
-- Registrar that they are unable to agree". (5): "If the Family Court has resolved a dispute about a child's name the Registrar must
-- assign or reassign the child's name in accordance with the court orders." (6), another court's determination: may.
DECLARE `Who settles the name` IS ONE OF
    `the name we chose stands`
    `the Registrar may assign a name`
    `the Registrar must follow the Family Court`

@export Can the Registrar give our child a name we did not choose (s 22(4)-(5))?
GIVEN `the Registrar thinks the name prohibited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is of the opinion that the name is a prohibited name
      `we lodged together and cannot agree` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether both parents lodged and satisfied the Registrar that they cannot agree on the name
      `the Family Court resolved the dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Family Court has resolved a dispute about the child's name
GIVETH A `Who settles the name`
DECIDE `Who settles our child's name` IS
    IF `the Family Court resolved the dispute` THEN `the Registrar must follow the Family Court`
    ELSE IF `the Registrar thinks the name prohibited` OR `we lodged together and cannot agree`
         THEN `the Registrar may assign a name`
         ELSE `the name we chose stands`

§§ `23 -- changing given names in the first year`

-- s 23(2): the application (b) "must be made within 12 months after the date of the child's birth"; (c) by (i) both parents if both
-- signed the statement, (ii) the one parent whose statement the Registrar accepted under s 15(1), (iii) the other parent if one has
-- since died, (iv) the guardian if both have since died, cannot be found or cannot exercise parental responsibility; (d) "cannot be
-- made if a previous application under this section has been made". (3): the Registrar may change the given names "unless a requested
-- name is a prohibited name". 12 months is read as to the same date a year later (an inference).
DECLARE `Who signed the statement` IS ONE OF
    `both parents signed`
    `one parent signed alone`

DECLARE `Who applies` IS ONE OF
    `both parents apply`
    `one parent applies`
    `the child's guardian applies`

@export Am I the right person to apply to change my child's given names (s 23(2)(c))?
GIVEN `who signed` IS A `Who signed the statement` @desc Who signed the child's birth registration statement
      `who applies` IS A `Who applies` @desc Who is applying for the change
      `one parent has since died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one of the parents has died since the statement was lodged
      `neither parent can act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether both parents have since died, cannot be found or cannot exercise parental responsibility
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I the right person to apply under s 23` IF
    CONSIDER `who applies`
    WHEN `the child's guardian applies` THEN `neither parent can act`
    WHEN `one parent applies` THEN
        (CONSIDER `who signed`
         WHEN `one parent signed alone` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE `one parent has since died`)
    OTHERWISE
        (CONSIDER `who signed`
         WHEN `both parents signed` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)

@export May I change my child's given names (s 23)?
GIVEN `the date of birth` IS A NUMBER @desc The child's date of birth, YYYYMMDD
      `the date of the application` IS A NUMBER @desc The date of the application, YYYYMMDD
      `the right person applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application is made by the person s 23(2)(c) names (see the question above)
      `applied under s 23 before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application to change the child's given names has been made before
      `the Registrar thinks a name prohibited` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is of the opinion that a requested name is a prohibited name
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I change my child's given names` IF
        `the date of the application` <= `the date of birth` + 10000
    AND `the right person applies`
    AND (NOT `applied under s 23 before`)
    AND (NOT `the Registrar thinks a name prohibited`)

§ `Part 6 -- registration of marriages`

-- s 37: "If a marriage is solemnized in the State, the marriage must be registered under this Act." s 38: "A person may have a marriage
-- registered by lodging with the Registrar a certificate of the marriage under the Marriage Act 1961 of the Commonwealth or, if the
-- marriage was solemnized before the commencement of that Act, the evidence of the marriage required by the Registrar." Note to s 38:
-- "Under section 50(4) of the Marriage Act 1961 of the Commonwealth the authorised celebrant is responsible for lodging the certificate".
-- s 39: registered by including the certificate, or its particulars, in the Register. Part 6 sets no time limit and puts no duty on
-- the couple.
DECLARE `Marriage document` IS ONE OF
    `a certificate under the Marriage Act 1961`
    `the evidence the Registrar requires`

@export Must our marriage be registered under this Act (s 37)?
GIVEN `solemnized in Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the marriage was solemnised in Western Australia
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must our marriage be registered` IF `solemnized in Western Australia`

@export What is lodged to register our marriage (s 38)?
GIVEN `solemnized before the Marriage Act 1961 commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the marriage was solemnised before the Commonwealth Marriage Act 1961 commenced
GIVETH A `Marriage document`
DECIDE `What is lodged to register our marriage` IS
    IF `solemnized before the Marriage Act 1961 commenced`
    THEN `the evidence the Registrar requires`
    ELSE `a certificate under the Marriage Act 1961`

§ `Part 7 -- registration of deaths`

§§ `40 -- must the death be registered here`

-- s 40(1): "If a person dies in the State, the death must be registered under this Act." (2): also on a State court's or a coroner's
-- order; (7): "The death of a stillborn child must be registered under this Act."
@export Must the death be registered under this Act (s 40(1), (2), (7))?
GIVEN `died in Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died in Western Australia
      `a State court or coroner ordered registration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a State court or a coroner in the State has ordered the death registered
      `a stillborn child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death is of a stillborn child
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the death be registered` IF
    `died in Western Australia` OR `a State court or coroner ordered registration` OR `a stillborn child`

-- s 40(4): a death "in an aircraft during a flight to an airport in the State" or "on a vessel during a voyage to a port in the State",
-- and (5) a death outside the Commonwealth of a person "domiciled or ordinarily resident in the State" or who "leaves property in the
-- State", MAY be registered; (6) "The Registrar may refuse to register a death under subsection (4) or (5) if the death is registered
-- under a corresponding law" -- the refusal is asked as an input.
@export May a death that happened elsewhere be registered here (s 40(4)-(6))?
GIVEN `died on a flight or voyage to Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died in an aircraft or vessel travelling to the State
      `died abroad, domiciled or resident or leaving property here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died outside Australia and was domiciled or ordinarily resident in the State, or left property in it
      `the Registrar refuses because it is registered elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar refuses because the death is registered under another State's law
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the death be registered here` IF
        (`died on a flight or voyage to Western Australia` OR `died abroad, domiciled or resident or leaving property here`)
    AND (NOT `the Registrar refuses because it is registered elsewhere`)

§§ `42, 43 -- who notifies the Registrar of the death, and by when`

-- s 42: if a person dies in the State, "(a) the funeral director or other person who arranges for the disposal of the person's remains;
-- or (b) the person who is in charge of an educational or scientific institution where the person's remains are placed for the purpose
-- of medical education or research, must, within 14 days of the date of death, finding of the person's body or placement of the
-- person's body, as is relevant to the case -- (c) notify the Registrar ... by lodging a death registration statement ...; and (d) give
-- the Registrar the certificate of cause of death". Penalty: $1 000. s 43: if a coroner at an inquest into a suspected death "finds that
-- the death has been established, the coroner is to be treated as the notifying person". Donating organs is not placing the remains
-- for medical education or research (an inference: s 42(b) speaks of the remains placed at an institution).
DECLARE `Death notifier` IS ONE OF
    `the funeral director or other person arranging disposal`
    `the person in charge of the institution`
    `the coroner`

@export Who must notify the Registrar of the death (ss 42, 43)?
GIVEN `the body was placed for medical education or research` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body was given to an educational or scientific institution for medical education or research
      `a coroner found a suspected death established` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner, at an inquest into a suspected death, found the death established
GIVETH A `Death notifier`
DECIDE `Who must notify the death` IS
    IF `a coroner found a suspected death established` THEN `the coroner`
    ELSE IF `the body was placed for medical education or research`
         THEN `the person in charge of the institution`
         ELSE `the funeral director or other person arranging disposal`

@export How many days does the notifier have to lodge the death registration statement (s 42)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days to notify the death` IS 14

@export Was the death notified in time (s 42)?
GIVEN `days after the death, finding or placement` IS A NUMBER @desc Days from the date of death (or finding or placement of the body) to the lodging of the statement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was the death notified in time` IF
    `days after the death, finding or placement` <= `How many days to notify the death`

§§ `44 -- the doctor's certificate of cause of death`

-- s 44(1): other than a stillbirth or neonatal death, the doctor who "(a) was responsible for the person's medical care immediately
-- before the death; or (b) examined the person's deceased body"; (2) a stillbirth: the doctor responsible "for the professional care of
-- the mother at the birth" or who examined the body; (3) a neonatal death: the doctor responsible for the child's care or who examined
-- the body -- "must, within 48 hours after the ... death -- (c) certify, in an approved form, the cause of the death; and (d) give the
-- certificate to the person who is responsible under section 42". Penalty: $1 000.
DECLARE `Kind of death` IS ONE OF
    `the death of a person`
    `a stillbirth`
    `a neonatal death`

DECLARE `Certifying doctor` IS ONE OF
    `the doctor who cared for the person, or examined the body`
    `the doctor who cared for the mother at the birth, or examined the body`
    `the doctor who cared for the child, or examined the body`
    `no certificate: the death is reported to the coroner`

-- s 44(5): "This section does not require a doctor to give a certificate of the cause of a person's death if the doctor is required
-- under section 17(3) of the Coroners Act 1996 to report the person's death to a coroner." Whether the death is reportable is a
-- borrowed term (Coroners Act 1996), asked as a fact.
@export Which doctor must certify the cause of death (s 44)?
GIVEN `the kind of death` IS A `Kind of death` @desc Whether it is the death of a person, a stillbirth or a neonatal death
      `the death must be reported to a coroner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor must report the death to a coroner under s 17(3) of the Coroners Act 1996
GIVETH A `Certifying doctor`
DECIDE `Which doctor must certify the cause of death` IS
    IF `the death must be reported to a coroner`
    THEN `no certificate: the death is reported to the coroner`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the kind of death`
         WHEN `a stillbirth` THEN `the doctor who cared for the mother at the birth, or examined the body`
         WHEN `a neonatal death` THEN `the doctor who cared for the child, or examined the body`
         OTHERWISE `the doctor who cared for the person, or examined the body`

-- s 44(4): no offence if "(a) a certificate required ... is given by another doctor; or (b) the doctor had reasonable cause for not
-- being able to comply". The reasonable cause is asked as an input.
@export Is the doctor in breach of the 48-hour duty to certify (s 44)?
GIVEN `the death must be reported to a coroner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor must report the death to a coroner
      `hours after the death` IS A NUMBER @desc Hours from the death to the certificate (or now, if none has been given)
      `another doctor gave the certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another doctor has given the certificate
      `the doctor had reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor had reasonable cause for not being able to comply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the doctor in breach of s 44` IF
        (NOT `the death must be reported to a coroner`)
    AND `hours after the death` > 48
    AND (NOT `another doctor gave the certificate`)
    AND (NOT `the doctor had reasonable cause`)

§§ `45-47 -- disposal of the remains`

-- s 4 "disposal": "(a) cremation of the remains; (b) burial of the remains (including burial at sea); (c) placing the remains in a
-- mausoleum or other permanent resting place; or (d) removal of the remains from the State (but not if the remains have been cremated or
-- are taken from the State by sea and buried at sea in the course of the voyage)".
DECLARE `What is done with the remains` IS ONE OF
    `cremation`
    `burial`
    `burial at sea`
    `placing in a mausoleum or other permanent resting place`
    `removal of the uncremated remains from the State`
    `removal of cremated remains from the State`
    `kept, not yet disposed of`

@export Is what is done with the remains a disposal under the Act (s 4)?
GIVEN `what is done` IS A `What is done with the remains` @desc What is done with the remains
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it a disposal` IF
    CONSIDER `what is done`
    WHEN `removal of cremated remains from the State` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `kept, not yet disposed of` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 45: the person arranging a disposal "other than by way of removal of the remains from the State, must within 7 days after the
-- disposal of the remains, give the Registrar notice". s 46: if remains "(other than cremated remains)" are removed from the State,
-- notice "(a) within 7 days after the removal ... and (b) within 28 days after the remains are disposed of outside the State". Penalty
-- for each: $1 000.
@export How many days after the disposal (or removal) does the funeral director have to notify the Registrar (ss 45, 46(a))?
GIVEN `what is done` IS A `What is done with the remains` @desc What is done with the remains
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days to notify the disposal` IS
    IF `Is it a disposal` `what is done` THEN 7 ELSE 0

@export Was the disposal notified in time (ss 45, 46(a))?
GIVEN `what is done` IS A `What is done with the remains` @desc What is done with the remains
      `days after the disposal` IS A NUMBER @desc Days from the disposal (or the removal from the State) to the notice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was the disposal notified in time` IF
    `Is it a disposal` `what is done` AND `days after the disposal` <= `How many days to notify the disposal` `what is done`

-- s 47: "If human remains have not been disposed of within 30 days after the date of death, the funeral director or other person who
-- has custody of the remains must give the Registrar notice". Disposal on day 30 is within 30 days.
@export Must the Registrar be told that the remains have not been disposed of (s 47)?
GIVEN `disposed of` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the remains have been disposed of
      `days since the death` IS A NUMBER @desc Days since the date of death
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Registrar be told the remains are not disposed of` IF
    (NOT `disposed of`) AND `days since the death` > 30

§§ `48 -- the Registrar registers the death`

-- s 48(1): "The Registrar is to register a death by making an entry about the death in the Register"; (2) on incomplete particulars;
-- (3) "even though the death is subject to an inquest or other coronial investigation and a finding has not been made about the cause of
-- death". The funeral director notifies (s 42); the Registrar registers (s 48).
DECLARE `Who registers` IS ONE OF
    `the Registrar`
    `the funeral director`
    `the doctor`
    `the family`

@export Who registers the death (s 48(1))?
GIVETH A `Who registers`
DECIDE `Who registers the death` IS `the Registrar`

@export May the death be registered before the coroner finds the cause (s 48(3))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the death be registered before the coroner finds the cause` IS TRUE

-- s 48(4): "A death certificate issued before the completion of an inquest or other coronial investigation into the cause of death must
-- be endorsed in a manner that the Registrar considers appropriate to indicate that fact."
@export Will the death certificate be marked as issued before the coronial investigation ended (s 48(4))?
GIVEN `a coronial investigation is incomplete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inquest or other coronial investigation into the cause of death is still incomplete
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Will the certificate be endorsed` IF
    `a coronial investigation is incomplete`

§ `Part 8 -- getting a certificate`

-- s 55(1)-(3): the Registrar "may, on application, search the Register"; "The applicant must state the reason for the applicant's
-- interest"; the Registrar "may reject the application if the applicant does not show an adequate reason". (4): regard to the
-- relationship between applicant and subject, the age and contents of the entry. s 57(1): "On completing a search of the Register the
-- Registrar may issue a certificate". Whether the reason is adequate, and whether to issue, are the Registrar's: asked as an input.
@export Can I be given a certified copy of the registration (ss 55, 57)?
GIVEN `I stated my reason for wanting it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you stated the reason for your interest in the entry
      `the Registrar accepts my reason as adequate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar accepts that you have shown an adequate reason and will issue
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be given a certificate` IF
    `I stated my reason for wanting it` AND `the Registrar accepts my reason as adequate`

§ `Fees -- Regulations r 4 and Act s 70`

-- Regulations r 4: "The fees set out in column 3 of the Table to this regulation are payable in respect of the corresponding matters
-- set out in column 2". The items a parent, a couple or a family meets (Table, from 1 Jul 2026):
--   1  certified copy of a registration (including one 10 year search)                                       60.00
--   3  commemorative certificate (including one 10 year search and a certified copy)                          70.00
--   4  certified copy of a registration at least 75 years old                                                 39.00
--   5  uncertified copy of a registration                                                                     25.00
--   6  each 10 year search other than under item 1 or 3                                                       36.00
--   7  application for registration of a change of name                                                     206.00
--   8  application for an addition to, or correction of, a registration (including one certified copy)        60.00
--   9  application to change a child's name within 12 months after the birth (including one certified copy)   60.00
--  11  priority processing of a certificate (in addition to the certificate fee)                              44.00
-- The Table lists no fee for lodging a birth registration statement, a marriage certificate or a death registration statement; so
-- registering costs nothing under the Regulations (an inference from the absence).
DECLARE `Registry service` IS ONE OF
    `registering the birth, marriage or death`
    `a certified copy of a registration`
    `a commemorative certificate`
    `a certified copy of a registration at least 75 years old`
    `an uncertified copy of a registration`
    `a further 10 year search`
    `registering a change of name`
    `adding to or correcting a registration`
    `changing a child's name within 12 months of birth`
    `priority processing of a certificate`

@export What is the prescribed fee for this service, in dollars (Regulations r 4)?
GIVEN `the service` IS A `Registry service` @desc The service you are asking for
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the fee` IS
    CONSIDER `the service`
    WHEN `registering the birth, marriage or death` THEN 0
    WHEN `a certified copy of a registration` THEN 60
    WHEN `a commemorative certificate` THEN 70
    WHEN `a certified copy of a registration at least 75 years old` THEN 39
    WHEN `an uncertified copy of a registration` THEN 25
    WHEN `a further 10 year search` THEN 36
    WHEN `registering a change of name` THEN 206
    WHEN `adding to or correcting a registration` THEN 60
    WHEN `changing a child's name within 12 months of birth` THEN 60
    WHEN `priority processing of a certificate` THEN 44

-- s 70: "The Registrar may remit the whole or part of a fee under this Act if the Registrar considers that in the circumstances it is
-- appropriate to do so." The amount remitted is the Registrar's, asked as an input.
@export What will I pay for this service, after any remission (Regulations r 4, Act s 70)?
GIVEN `the service` IS A `Registry service` @desc The service you are asking for
      `with priority processing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you ask for the certificate to be processed in priority (item 11, added to the fee)
      `the amount the Registrar remits` IS A NUMBER @desc The dollars of the fee the Registrar remits under s 70 (0 if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What will I pay` IS
    LET gross MEANS `What is the fee` `the service`
                    + (IF `with priority processing` THEN `What is the fee` `priority processing of a certificate` ELSE 0)
    IN IF gross - `the amount the Registrar remits` < 0 THEN 0 ELSE gross - `the amount the Registrar remits`

§ `Penalties`

-- ss 12(2), 16(1), 42, 44, 45, 46 and 47: "Penalty: $1 000." for each.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `late birth notice under s 12`
    `late birth registration under s 16`
    `late death notification under s 42`
    `late cause of death certificate under s 44`
    `late disposal notice under s 45 or 46`
    `no notice of undisposed remains under s 47`

@export What is the most the person responsible can be fined for this offence, in dollars?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most that can be fined` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `late birth notice under s 12` THEN 1000
    WHEN `late birth registration under s 16` THEN 1000
    WHEN `late death notification under s 42` THEN 1000
    WHEN `late cause of death certificate under s 44` THEN 1000
    WHEN `late disposal notice under s 45 or 46` THEN 1000
    WHEN `no notice of undisposed remains under s 47` THEN 1000
